WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:04.608
To run a process line, you mainly measure four variables: pressure,

2
00:00:04.782 --> 00:00:06.442
temperature, flow and level.

3
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.329
On this line there is a meter for each.

4
00:00:10.354 --> 00:00:12.096
Pressure is force per area.

5
00:00:12.816 --> 00:00:17.809
You measure it in bar, or in kilopascals: 1 bar is 100 kilopascals.

6
00:00:18.523 --> 00:00:22.006
American equipment often shows pounds per square inch,

7
00:00:22.181 --> 00:00:25.374
and 1 bar is about 14.5 of those.

8
00:00:26.705 --> 00:00:28.864
After the pump sits a pressure gauge.

9
00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:31.243
Like almost every gauge,

10
00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:35.423
it shows gauge pressure: the pressure relative to the air around it.

11
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:37.903
You write that as bar gauge.

12
00:00:38.934 --> 00:00:43.299
The absolute pressure counts from a perfect vacuum, in bar absolute.

13
00:00:44.082 --> 00:00:47.449
At sea level the air itself presses about 1 bar.

14
00:00:48.165 --> 00:00:52.681
So if the gauge reads 2 bar, the absolute pressure is about 3 bar.

15
00:00:53.678 --> 00:00:57.451
So vacuum shows up in two ways: as negative gauge pressure,

16
00:00:57.625 --> 00:00:59.262
or as low absolute pressure.

17
00:01:00.013 --> 00:01:03.693
Half a bar below the outside air is minus half a bar gauge,

18
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:05.702
or about half a bar absolute.

19
00:01:06.447 --> 00:01:09.188
When someone quotes a pressure, ask which one.

20
00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.258
Then flow.

21
00:01:11.989 --> 00:01:16.632
That is volume per time, such as cubic metres per hour or litres per minute.

22
00:01:17.397 --> 00:01:21.031
Or mass per time, such as kilograms or tonnes per hour.

23
00:01:22.053 --> 00:01:26.952
The mass flow is the volume flow times the density: the mass of one cubic metre.

24
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.916
Water weighs about 1,000 kilograms per cubic metre,

25
00:01:32.032 --> 00:01:37.245
so one cubic metre of water per hour is about 1,000 kilograms per hour.

26
00:01:37.986 --> 00:01:46.136
A syrup of 1,250 kilograms per cubic metre gives 1,250 kilograms per hour.

27
00:01:47.211 --> 00:01:50.125
Recipes and balances work in kilograms.

28
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:54.699
So always ask what a meter really shows: volume or mass.

29
00:01:56.016 --> 00:01:58.071
There are many kinds of flow meter.

30
00:01:58.817 --> 00:02:03.275
A magnetic flow meter measures the speed of a liquid that conducts electricity.

31
00:02:03.965 --> 00:02:06.844
So on very pure water or on oil it fails.

32
00:02:07.913 --> 00:02:10.653
This meter in the pipe is a Coriolis meter.

33
00:02:11.357 --> 00:02:15.258
It vibrates a tube and reads the twist the flowing mass causes.

34
00:02:15.952 --> 00:02:19.783
So it measures mass directly, to about 0.1 percent.

35
00:02:20.519 --> 00:02:22.562
But it costs thousands of euros.

36
00:02:23.627 --> 00:02:27.586
A vortex meter counts the vortices behind an obstacle in the pipe,

37
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.223
and so reads the speed.

38
00:02:29.958 --> 00:02:33.905
A differential-pressure meter reads the pressure drop across a restriction.

39
00:02:34.922 --> 00:02:38.730
And every meter is only as good as its last calibration:

40
00:02:38.881 --> 00:02:40.762
the check against a known reference.

41
00:02:42.087 --> 00:02:42.749
Then level.

42
00:02:43.457 --> 00:02:46.522
You measure it with a pressure sensor at the bottom of the vat,

43
00:02:46.696 --> 00:02:50.656
as here, with a radar that measures from above, or with a float on the liquid.

44
00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.511
A pressure sensor really measures the weight of the column of liquid above it.

45
00:02:56.255 --> 00:02:59.854
If the density differs from what it is set for, the height is wrong.

46
00:03:00.588 --> 00:03:03.014
The mass that follows from it is still right.

47
00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.032
Every principle has a weak spot.

48
00:03:06.779 --> 00:03:10.390
Foam can fool a radar, and sticky product can jam a float.

49
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.030
If a level reads oddly, ask what principle the meter uses.

50
00:03:16.343 --> 00:03:18.781
An example with this vat’s pressure sensor.

51
00:03:19.529 --> 00:03:23.789
It is set up for water, 1,000 kilograms per cubic metre.

52
00:03:24.853 --> 00:03:31.656
Now it holds syrup of 1,250 kilograms per cubic metre, really 2.0 metres deep.

53
00:03:32.381 --> 00:03:36.247
The pressure at the bottom is the density times gravity times the height:

54
00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:40.090
24,525 pascals.

55
00:03:41.068 --> 00:03:46.571
The meter divides that pressure by the density of water, and shows 2.5 metres.

56
00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:51.084
Half a metre too high: the high-level alarm may trip early,

57
00:03:51.293 --> 00:03:53.267
and the tank looks fuller than it is.

58
00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:58.820
The mass the meter implies is still right, in a tank with straight walls.

59
00:03:59.501 --> 00:04:03.321
So enter the right density, or read the tank in kilograms.

60
00:04:04.636 --> 00:04:05.902
Finally, temperature.

61
00:04:06.626 --> 00:04:10.028
A difference of one degree Celsius equals one kelvin,

62
00:04:10.179 --> 00:04:12.826
the scale that starts at absolute zero.

63
00:04:13.551 --> 00:04:16.825
So from 20 to 80 degrees adds 60 kelvin,

64
00:04:17.069 --> 00:04:21.167
and heat sums with differences may simply use degrees Celsius.

65
00:04:22.166 --> 00:04:27.019
After the heat exchanger a Pt100 measures the temperature of the juice.

66
00:04:27.728 --> 00:04:31.745
It is a platinum resistor of 100 ohms at 0 degrees,

67
00:04:31.966 --> 00:04:36.111
accurate to a few tenths of a degree at ordinary process temperatures.

68
00:04:37.105 --> 00:04:41.819
The other common sensor is a thermocouple: two different metals joined at the tip.

69
00:04:42.527 --> 00:04:45.070
It has a wider range, but is less accurate.

70
00:04:46.149 --> 00:04:48.866
The sensor sits in a thermowell in the pipe.

71
00:04:49.607 --> 00:04:54.239
In a slow flow it can lag by minutes, and the control reacts too late.
